window.NEWS = {"issues":[{"id":"2026-22","season":2026,"week":22,"date":"2026-08-31","phase":"regular season","articles":[{"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","desk":"The Bright Side","color":"#E8B04B","headline":"The Diamond Shines Brightest Under Pressure!","dek":"Tough breaks build championship grit, and our playoff contenders are showing unbelievable heart down the stretch.","body":"Keep your heads high, everyone, because baseball is a game of beautiful tomorrows! When the scoreboard looks stormy, that just means the rainbow is right around the corner. Every bump in the road is simply character development on the way to greatness!\n\nTake Heidi, for instance. Sure, the final tally against Andrew reads 17-4-1, but let us look at the glorious foundation underneath! Heidi claimed triples in a thrilling 1-0 nail-biter, captured saves, dominated strikeout-to-walk ratio, and took the error category. Most importantly, GIRL ON FIRE remains comfortably in fifth place at 215-204-65, holding tight to a playoff spot with just one week to go! Plus, the Minnesota Twins called up top prospect Walker Jenkins on Friday. The seeds of triumph are already planted, Heidi, and October glory loves an underdog story!\n\nOver in the matchup of the week, Austin gave us an absolute masterpiece of competitive spirit. Yes, Barak took a 13-9 victory behind Rafael Devers and his 12 hits, but Austin put up a dazzling 121 grade that would have beaten seven other teams in the league! Talk about phenomenal effort! Cal Raleigh turned into a real-life superhero by crushing five home runs. On the mound, Chris Sale delivered nine shutout innings with 11 strikeouts on Thursday, followed immediately by Michael Wacha tossing eight scoreless frames with 10 punchouts on Friday. \n\nAustin dropped walk total 24 to 25, extra-base hits 22 to 23, wins 4 to 5, holds 1 to 2, and barely missed ERA (2.76 to 2.78) and WHIP (1.06 to 1.07). That is eight straight matchup losses on paper, but in reality, Austin is playing top-tier baseball at the perfect time. A bounce here or there and that scoreboard flips right upside down!\n\nMeanwhile, Andrew vaulted into first place at 258-174-52 with Pete Crow-Armstrong going completely wild. PCA smashed five home runs and drove in eight runs, highlighted by a magical three-homer Saturday! But do not count out Benny, who sits just two games back in second place after a hard-fought 7-12-3 duel with Jacob. Benny still captured home runs 7 to 6 behind Luis Garcia Jr. and took saves 3 to 2. The reigning two-time champ has Max Fried back from the injured list and plenty of fight left!\n\nAnd how about Ben? Clinging to the sixth and final playoff seed, Ben squeezed out a gritty 10-9-3 triumph over Todd, taking quality starts 7 to 6 and triples 1 to 0 behind Drew Rasmussen's two stellar outings. One week left, everybody. Keep smiling, keep believing, and swing for the fences!","bit":{"type":"trivia","name":"Rosie's Trivia","data":{"question":"Who went first overall in the 2024 draft, and which manager took him?","last_q":null,"last_a":null},"text":"Welcome to the very first edition of Rosie's Trivia! I am so excited to test your league memories. Here is your inaugural question: Who went first overall in the 2024 draft, and which manager took him? Check back next week for the answer and a brand-new stumper!"}},{"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","desk":"The Numbers Desk","color":"#7FA8D6","headline":"A Masterclass in Distributional Cruelty","dek":"Barak and Austin put on the matchup of the year while the sixth seed hangs by two categories.","body":"Welcome to Week 22, where the sample sizes are finally large enough to hurt your feelings.\n\nLet us begin with Austin, who managed an overall performance grade of 121 and an all-play record of 7-2-0. In seven out of nine alternate universes this week, Austin wins handily and halts his losing skid. Instead, he drew Barak, who posted a league-best grade of 136 and swept the entire room at 9-0-0. Barak took the matchup 13-9-0, handing Austin his eighth consecutive defeat.\n\nWhat makes this mathematically exquisite is the sheer density of knife-edge variance. Barak secured walks 25 to 24, extra-base hits 23 to 22, holds 2 to 1, and wins 5 to 4. Austin took ERA 2.76 to 2.78 and WHIP 1.06 to 1.07. That is eight categories decided by the thinnest sliver of statistical noise. Compounding the pain: Austin left a pitching win on his bench, directly forfeiting a tie in that category. He also left all four weekly transactions unused. The math will forgive bad luck, Austin, but it will never forgive unforced errors.\n\nMeanwhile, the battle for the final playoff ticket between Ben and Todd came down to the absolute margins. Ben edged Todd 10-9-3, driven by a 7 to 6 advantage in quality starts and a single triple (1 to 0). Todd captured OPS by .005 (.713 to .708) and strikeout-to-walk ratio by a rounding error (3.06 to 2.93), but it was not enough. Ben now sits two full games ahead of Todd for the sixth and final seed entering the regular season finale.\n\nAt the summit, the theoretical hierarchy has shifted. Benny stumbled against Jacob, losing 12-7-3 behind a dismal 74 pitching grade. Jacob rode 14 scoreless innings from Jesús Luzardo and Eduardo Rodriguez to a 136 pitching grade, denying Benny the top spot he had occupied for 13 weeks.\n\nAndrew pounced immediately, dismantling Heidi 17-4-1 behind Pete Crow-Armstrong's five-homer explosion. Heidi managed a grade of 58 and an 0-9-0 all-play showing, offering zero resistance as Andrew claimed first place by two categories.","bit":{"type":"odds","name":"The Odds Board","data":{"odds":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":64.3,"title":32.2},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":75.3,"title":45.7},"Jacob":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":25.4,"title":10.9},"Brian":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":18.6,"title":6.1},"Heidi":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":10.0,"title":3.4},"Ben":{"po":82.8,"bye":0.0,"final":5.5,"title":1.6},"Austin":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Todd":{"po":17.2,"bye":0.0,"final":0.8,"title":0.2},"Barak":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Gabe":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0}},"prev":{},"phase":"regular season"},"text":"The regular season ends in seven days, and our 2,000 Monte Carlo simulations have officially locked Andrew and Benny into first-round byes at 100.0%. The biggest mover is Ben, whose narrow victory over Todd vaulted his playoff probability to 82.8%, leaving Todd with just a 17.2% prayer. The most surprising number on the board belongs to Jacob: despite sitting comfortably in third place with a 100% playoff lock, his odds to win the Frank Cup sit at only 10.9%, severely dampened by an offense that grades out in the bottom quartile far too frequently."}},{"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","desk":"The Roast Pit","color":"#D95B43","headline":"The Hospital Ward Surrenders First Place","dek":"Benny benches reality, Austin masters the art of losing, and Heidi forgets the season is happening.","body":"Welcome to the penultimate week of the regular season, where the playoff stakes are higher than ever and several managers have apparently thrown their phones into a lake.\n\nBenny had occupied the league throne for thirteen weeks this year, acting as if a third straight title was preordained. Then Jacob showed up with a 112 grade and handed Benny a crisp 12-7-3 reality check. How did the two-time champion respond to the pressure? By operating a mobile trauma clinic. Benny started Joshua Báez and J.T. Ginn for seven full days while both were on the injured list, along with Emerson Hancock, Pete Fairbanks, and George Klassen. Benny sat on three unused adds while Jacob walked away with twelve categories. The crown has officially slipped.\n\nTaking over the top spot is Andrew, who feasted on Heidi in a 17-4-1 demolition. Andrew's squad was firing on all cylinders behind Pete Crow-Armstrong, while Heidi authored an all-time stinker: a league-worst 58 grade and a clean 0-9-0 all-play sweep. Heidi accomplished this masterclass by starting Shohei Ohtani's batting slot and Ceddanne Rafaela for seven days of injured inactivity, alongside Byron Buxton, Chase Burns, and Christian Scott. Naturally, Heidi burned zero adds and lost Quality Starts 4 to 3. If you do not want to set a lineup, you can just say so.\n\nMeanwhile, Austin has extended his losing streak to eight straight weeks, which requires serious dedication. In fairness, Austin put up a 121 grade, but he still lost 13-9 to Barak. The real tragedy is that Austin left all four adds on the table while dropping categories by a whisker: walks (24 to 25), extra-base hits (22 to 23), and holds (1 to 2). Best of all, Austin left a win, a quality start, and 11 strikeouts rotting on his bench, losing Wins 4 to 5. That is an artisan bench blunder.\n\nFinally, Todd fell 9-10-3 to Ben after Seth Lugo completely nuked Todd's ratios. Todd kept all four adds unused and lost Quality Starts by a count of 7 to 6. Gabe did the exact same thing against Brian, hoarding four adds and starting Brent Rooker on the IL all week to secure a 13-6-3 loss. Week 23 is your last chance to pretend you care.","bit":{"type":"dud","name":"Dud of the Week","data":{"name":"Seth Lugo","mlb":"KC","owner":"Todd","line":"3.2 IP, 1 L, 2 K, 22.09 ERA, 3.00 WHIP"},"text":"This week's Dud of the Week trophy belongs to Seth Lugo, owned by Todd. Lugo took the mound on Sunday and authored 3.2 innings of pure agony, handing his owner a 22.09 ERA, a 3.00 WHIP, and a loss with just two strikeouts. Thanks in large part to Lugo serving up batting practice, Todd dropped his matchup to Ben by a razor-thin 10-9-3 margin."}},{"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","desk":"Matchups to Watch","color":"#9A8FB8","headline":"The Final Chimes Before the Ground Gives Way","dek":"One week remains in the regular season, and the burial plots are nearly fully occupied.","body":"The air grows thin, cold, and heavy with the scent of freshly turned earth. Twenty-two weeks of toil have brought this league to the lip of the grave. Week 23 is the final hour of the regular season. For four souls, the casket is already being lowered. For several others, one misstep on the wet grass will plunge them straight into the darkness.\n\nConsider Benny, who sat comfortably on the throne for thirteen weeks before Jacob handed him a 7-12-3 defeat, knocking him out of first place. Now Benny faces Barak, who arrives smelling of sulfur after posting a league-best 136 grade and a 9-0-0 all-play showing behind Rafael Devers. Benny holds the historical edge, having inflicted a gruesome 16-2-4 butcher job upon Barak in Week 5 of this very season. But past triumphs cannot warm cold hands. Barak has life, and Benny is suddenly bleeding.\n\nAt the edge of the playoff cliff stands Ben, clinging to the sixth and final postseason berth. Right above him is Heidi, bruised and staggering after Andrew demolished her 17-4-1. They collide with everything on the line. Their shared past contains dark memories: back in Week 4 of 2025, Ben delivered an ungodly 17-2-3 desecration to Heidi. If Heidi repeats her Week 22 showing, where she managed a meager 58 grade, Ben will happily arrange her final services.\n\nTodd is not dead yet, but he is running out of oxygen. Sitting in seventh place, seven category victories behind Ben, Todd needs an absolute reckoning against Jacob to avoid missing the cut. Todd does hold a 7-2 lifetime advantage over Jacob, highlighted by a 17-4-1 massacre in Week 1 of 2025. Yet Jacob just dismantled Benny with ferocious pitching from Jesus Luzardo and Eduardo Rodriguez. If Todd trots out starts like Seth Lugo's 22.09 ERA disaster from Sunday, the dirt will cover him before Friday.\n\nThen there is Andrew, reigning atop the mountain at 258-174-52 after Pete Crow-Armstrong blasted three home runs in a single afternoon. Andrew draws Gabe, who occupies the basement at 4-18-0. In Week 5, Andrew handed Gabe a humiliating 16-2-4 beatdown. Gabe left four transactions untouched while losing three categories by a hair last week. It is difficult to resurrect a corpse that refuses to breathe.","bit":{"type":"matchups","name":"Upset Alert","data":[{"a":"Benny","b":"Barak","kind":"regular season","form_a":"L 7-12, W 10-8, W 13-7","form_b":"W 13-9, W 12-9, L 6-13","grade_a":112,"grade_b":98,"n":10,"rec":"Benny 7, Barak 2, 1 tied","worst":"Benny won 16-2-4 (2026 week 5)","last":"Barak won 11-8-3 (2026 week 14)"},{"a":"Austin","b":"Brian","kind":"regular season","form_a":"L 9-13, L 8-10, L 7-12","form_b":"W 13-6, W 11-9, L 6-15","grade_a":90,"grade_b":104,"n":8,"rec":"Austin 3, Brian 4, 1 tied","worst":"Austin won 15-6-1 (2023 week 14)","last":"Austin won 14-6-2 (2026 week 14)"},{"a":"Andrew","b":"Gabe","kind":"regular season","form_a":"W 17-4, L 9-11, W 11-8","form_b":"L 6-13, L 9-12, W 12-7","grade_a":112,"grade_b":81,"n":12,"rec":"Andrew 6, Gabe 6","worst":"Andrew won 16-2-4 (2026 week 5)","last":"Andrew won 16-4-2 (2026 week 14)"},{"a":"Todd","b":"Jacob","kind":"regular season","form_a":"L 9-10, W 11-9, W 15-6","form_b":"W 12-7, L 9-10, L 8-11","grade_a":96,"grade_b":107,"n":9,"rec":"Todd 7, Jacob 2","worst":"Todd won 17-4-1 (2025 week 1)","last":"Todd won 9-8-5 (2026 week 14)"},{"a":"Heidi","b":"Ben","kind":"regular season","form_a":"L 4-17, L 9-11, W 13-6","form_b":"W 10-9, W 10-9, L 7-13","grade_a":102,"grade_b":98,"n":11,"rec":"Heidi 4, Ben 5, 2 tied","worst":"Ben won 17-2-3 (2025 week 4)","last":"Heidi won 11-8-3 (2026 week 14)"}],"text":"Upset Alert: Barak over Benny. Barak just routed Austin behind a pristine 136 grade while Benny stumbled through neglected rosters and dormant waiver wire allowances. The former champion is staggering toward the bye week, and Barak arrives carrying a very sharp shovel."}},{"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","desk":"The Insider","color":"#6FB38A","headline":"COUP AT THE TOP AS BENNY TUMBLERS AND AUSTIN CRUMBLES","dek":"Andrew seizes first place while Austin leaves a critical victory on the pine during an eight-week slide.","body":"The walls inside the commissioner's war room are practically vibrating. Multiple league sources confirmed late Sunday night that Benny has officially been dethroned from first place after spending thirteen weeks perched at the summit. The culprit? A brutal 7-12-3 dismantling at the hands of Jacob. Front office insiders report total panic after Benny left three unused waiver adds on the table while trotting out ghost lineups. Joshua Báez and J.T. Ginn were both lodged in active spots for seven full days while on the injured list, alongside appearances from Pete Fairbanks, Emerson Hancock, and George Klassen. Dropping Fairbanks right after Miami shut him down with nerve irritation did not stop the bleeding.\n\nThat throne now belongs to Andrew, whose clubhouse is popping corks following a 17-4-1 demolition of Heidi. Whispers out of Chicago claim Pete Crow-Armstrong is seeing beach balls after blasting five home runs this week, capped by a monstrous four-hit, three-homer, six-RBI outburst on Saturday. Andrew shrugged off horrific disaster starts from Jacob deGrom (19.64 ERA) and Zack Wheeler (18.00 ERA) because Heidi's pitching completely cratered. Matthew Liberatore was touched up for an 18.00 ERA and Andrew Abbott bled a 21.00 ERA over three frames. To make matters worse, Heidi left all four weekly adds sitting on the wire while dropping Quality Starts by a painful 4 to 3 margin.\n\nMeanwhile, the mood in Austin's camp has turned strictly apocalyptic. A team insider described Sunday evening as a funeral without the flower arrangements. Austin has now dropped eight consecutive matchups after Barak snatched a 13-9-0 victory. Austin rolled out absolute masterpieces from Chris Sale (nine scoreless innings, 11 strikeouts) and Michael Wacha (eight scoreless, 10 strikeouts), yet lost the matchup because of bench malpractice. Austin left one win sitting on his bench and ended up dropping the category 5 to 4. That was not all. Barak edged out walks 25 to 24, extra-base hits 23 to 22, and holds 2 to 1, while Austin burned zero of his four allocated waiver pickups.\n\nOver in the playoff bubble dogfight, Ben snatched a vital 10-9-3 triumph over Todd to cling tightly to the sixth and final seed heading into the regular season finale. A source close to Todd revealed heavy regret over four unused waiver moves after dropping quality starts 7 to 6 and triples 1 to 0.","bit":{"type":"rumor","name":"Trade Rumor of the Week","data":{"a":"Gabe","b":"Heidi","a_needs":"SV","b_needs":"A","a_gives":"Ozzie Albies","b_gives":"Emilio Pagán","why":"Gabe ranks 7% in SV over the last four weeks while Heidi sits at 75%; Heidi is at 29% in A where Gabe is at 89% (share of all-play category wins)."},"text":"TRADE RUMOR OF THE WEEK: The August 6 deadline is long gone, but front office spies are already buzzing about potential offseason keeper swaps between Gabe and Heidi. Gabe has managed a pathetic 7 percent win rate in saves over the last month, while Heidi boasts a 75 percent mark. Conversely, Heidi sits at 29 percent in assists compared to Gabe's league-best 89 percent. The chatter in the hotel lobbies? A winter blockbuster sending second baseman Ozzie Albies to Heidi in exchange for bullpen arm Emilio Pagán to solve both rosters before 2027."}}],"roster":["rosie","norm","seymour","doug","sauce"]},{"id":"2026-23","season":2026,"week":23,"date":"2026-09-07","phase":"regular season finale; the playoff field is now set","articles":[{"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","desk":"The Bright Side","color":"#E8B04B","headline":"The Best Is Yet to Come as Playoff Magic Begins!","dek":"A thrilling regular season finale brings playoff berths, character-building moments, and plenty of reasons to smile!","body":"What a majestic week of fantasy baseball! The regular season finale brought all the passion, heart, and grit we could ever ask for! While some doors closed and others opened, every single manager in The Frank Cup Gazette league gave us something to cheer about.\n\nLet us start with Benny! Now, some folks might look at a 17-3-2 victory by Barak and focus on the score. But I see a champion playing the long game! Benny clinched the number two seed and earned a glorious first-round playoff bye! Michael King was absolute perfection with 12.0 scoreless innings, two wins, and nine strikeouts! Sure, a few injuries pooled up on the roster, but a week off to rest and regroup is precisely what the doctor ordered. Benny is right where he needs to be to chase another title!\n\nAnd how about Austin? Last week, my colleagues were wringing their hands over his bad luck. But I told you the tide was bound to turn, and boy did it ever! Austin stormed back with an emphatic 14-6-2 victory over Brian! Behind solid pitching and nine hitting category wins, Austin proved that true spirit never gives up. Missing the playoffs is just a setup for an unbelievable comeback story next spring!\n\nSpeaking of pure heart, Todd put on an absolute clinic despite coming up just short against Jacob, 11-9-2! Todd posted a phenomenal 123 overall grade, fueled by Bryan Woo’s heroic eight scoreless innings with nine strikeouts on Sunday. It takes a truly special squad to put up numbers like that! The ball did not bounce Todd's way on a few razor-thin categories like steals and holds, but that 123 grade proves his foundation is rock solid!\n\nCongratulations are also in order for Ben and Heidi! Ben secured the sixth and final playoff spot with a spirited 13-7-2 win, backed by Ronald Acuña Jr. smashing three home runs and driving in nine runs! Meanwhile, Heidi locked up the fifth seed and is officially postseason bound!\n\nThe postseason stage is set, friends! Keep those spirits high, support your team, and remember: every champion was once a contender who refused to give up!","bit":{"type":"trivia","name":"Rosie's Trivia","data":{"question":"Which manager has beaten Austin the most times in the regular season?","last_q":"Who went first overall in the 2024 draft, and which manager took him?","last_a":"Ronald Acuña Jr., taken by Jacob."},"text":"Rosie's Trivia Answer: Last week's answer was Ronald Acuña Jr., selected first overall in 2024 by Jacob! This week's question: Which manager has beaten Austin the most times in the regular season?"}},{"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","desk":"The Numbers Desk","color":"#7FA8D6","headline":"The Calculus of Elimination: How the Math Settled the Field","dek":"Twenty-three weeks of data have finally extinguished all remaining regular season variance.","body":"The regular season has concluded, and with it, the luxury of arguing with arithmetic. The sample size is no longer an approximation; it is twenty-three weeks wide, and it offers zero sympathy for those on the wrong side of the distribution.\n\nLet us begin with the battle for the final postseason berth. Last week, our model assigned Ben an 82.8% probability to secure the sixth seed over Todd's 17.2%. Ben handled his business with a clinical 13-7-2 victory over Heidi, riding a 142 hitting grade behind Ronald Acuña Jr. and Randy Arozarena. Heidi made this rather effortless by posting a league-worst grade of 63 and a pristine 0-9-0 all-play mark.\n\nTodd, meanwhile, suffered the week's sharpest distributional cruelty. He generated a 123 overall grade, an all-play record of 7-2-0, and Bryan Woo's magnificent 14 scoreless innings. In seven out of nine potential pairings, Todd wins comfortably. Instead, he drew Jacob (112 grade) and lost 9-11-2. The margins were microscopically agonizing: Todd dropped stolen bases 4 to 5, holds 1 to 2, WHIP 1.15 to 1.13, and quality starts 7 to 8. To compound the inefficiency, Todd left all four weekly transactions unused while dropping four categories by a single unit.\n\nAt the summit, Andrew claimed the regular season championship in commanding fashion, dismantling Gabe 15-6-1 behind a 126 grade and an 8-1-0 all-play showing. Franzia swiped 16 bases, establishing the third-highest single-week total in league history. Andrew concludes the regular season with a 273-180-53 category record and a league-leading season grade of 113, aided by a league-best +14 category wins in schedule luck.\n\nBenny, who occupied first place for thirteen weeks, limped into the second seed after being obliterated 3-17-2 by Barak. Barak recorded a 135 grade on the back of Rafael Devers, while Benny managed a 64 grade. Benny's managerial execution remained questionable to the end: he started three injured players, left three adds unused, and lost holds by one while leaving a hold on his bench.\n\nThe final six-team bracket is now locked. The math does not care about your feelings, but it will gladly calculate your postseason demise.","bit":{"type":"odds","name":"The Odds Board","data":{"odds":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":59.5,"title":26.2},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":76.5,"title":50.5},"Jacob":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":30.3,"title":12.7},"Brian":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":17.2,"title":5.8},"Heidi":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":11.2,"title":3.5},"Ben":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":5.2,"title":1.3},"Austin":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Todd":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Barak":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Gabe":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0}},"prev":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":64.3,"title":32.2},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":75.3,"title":45.7},"Jacob":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":25.4,"title":10.9},"Brian":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":18.6,"title":6.1},"Heidi":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":10.0,"title":3.4},"Ben":{"po":82.8,"bye":0.0,"final":5.5,"title":1.6},"Austin":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Todd":{"po":17.2,"bye":0.0,"final":0.8,"title":0.2},"Barak":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Gabe":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0}},"phase":"regular season finale; the playoff field is now set"},"text":"Andrew enters the postseason as the heavy favorite at 50.5% title odds (up from 45.7%), holding a massive 76.5% probability of reaching the final. Benny's title probability tumbled from 32.2% to 26.2% following his blowout loss to Barak. The most surprising output is Jacob climbing to 12.7% title odds despite failing to secure a bye; his second-ranked pitching floor makes him a statistical landmine for any opponent in a short three-week sprint."}},{"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","desk":"The Roast Pit","color":"#D95B43","headline":"Benny Punts the Crown and Todd Sleepwalks into Winter","dek":"A regular season finale defined by neglected IL slots, hoarded waiver adds, and historic collapses.","body":"Last week I roasted Benny for treating his fantasy roster like a trauma ICU and coughing up first place after thirteen glorious weeks. You would think a two-time champion might look in the mirror, check an injury report, and fight for the top seed in the finale. Instead, Benny turned in an artistic masterpiece of incompetence: a 17-3-2 humiliation courtesy of Barak.\n\nBenny registered a league-worst 64 grade, went 1-8-0 in all-play, and left three adds completely untouched on the table. To sweeten the disaster, he started Joshua Báez, Gage Jump, and J.T. Ginn while all three were on the injured list, and lost holds 1-0 because his lone hold of the week was stranded harmlessly on his bench. Doug Mercer called the upset last week, but even Doug could not have anticipated Benny folding this quickly.\n\nSpeaking of managers who apparently uninstalled the Yahoo app during the most important week of the season, Todd missed the playoff cut in heartbreakingly stupid fashion. Todd put up a 123 grade against Jacob, but dropped an 11-9-2 decision because he refused to use a single one of his four available adds. Jacob edged him in quality starts 8 to 7, holds 2 to 1, stolen bases 5 to 4, and WHIP 1.13 to 1.15. That is four separate categories lost by the thinnest margin imaginable while four roster moves sat collecting dust. Having Kyle Harrison surrender 13 earned runs in 7.1 innings and watching Matt Olson go 1-for-24 certainly did not help Todd's cause either.\n\nThen we have Heidi, who stumbled into the postseason purely because the bottom four teams were even more allergic to winning. Heidi earned an all-play record of 0-9-0 with an abysmal 63 grade in a 13-7-2 thumping by Ben. How do you prepare for the postseason? If you are Heidi, you leave all four adds on the wire and start Ceddanne Rafaela and Shohei Ohtani's hitter card for seven full days while both were parked on the injured list. Ben happily walked away with four wins and four quality starts to lock down the sixth seed.\n\nAt least Andrew did not sleepwalk through the finish line. Andrew locked up the regular season crown by crushing Gabe 15-6-1 behind a vintage 16-steal blitz, the third-highest single-week total in league history. Andrew did manage to lose runs batted in and wins by a single tally while benching two RBIs and a win, but when you face Gabe's 51-grade pitching staff, you can afford to manage with your eyes closed.","bit":{"type":"dud","name":"Dud of the Week","data":{"name":"Kyle Harrison","mlb":"MIL","owner":"Todd","line":"7.1 IP, 1 L, 7 K, 15.95 ERA, 3.41 WHIP"},"text":"Dud of the Week: Kyle Harrison (MIL), owned by Todd. Harrison tossed 7.1 innings of pure batting practice, posting a grotesque 15.95 ERA and a 3.41 WHIP. If you ever wanted to know what an active sabotage looks like during a playoff push, Harrison just provided the textbook blueprint."}},{"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","desk":"Matchups to Watch","color":"#9A8FB8","headline":"The Casket Latches Shut on Four, Opens for Two More","dek":"The regular season has expired, four corpses are buried, and the playoff gauntlet offers only fresh graves.","body":"The soil has settled on the regular season. For Todd, Barak, Austin, and Gabe, the dirt is packed tight, the eulogies are finished, and the long, freezing winter has arrived. As I warned seven days ago, Barak rose from the dead only to drag Benny down into the pit with him. Benny was dismantled in an ungodly 17-3-2 massacre, yielding the regular season crown to Andrew. Benny escapes to a first-round bye, but he arrives in the parlor battered, bandaged, and carrying a 64 grade that smells like rot.\n\nFor the four souls condemned to the opening round of the postseason, there is no solace. Survival is temporary. Ruin is inevitable.\n\n**No. 3 Jacob vs. No. 6 Ben**\n\nJacob crawled into this matchup after surviving Todd 11-9-2, a duel where Todd actually out-graded him 123 to 112. Now Jacob must confront his personal grim reaper. Ben enters the postseason on a three-matchup winning streak, having just suffocated Heidi 13-7-2 behind Ronald Acuña Jr. and Randy Arozarena. Ben has claimed eight of their twelve lifetime meetings. Jacob knows the exact weight of the shovel Ben carries: nobody in the league has forgotten Week 12 of 2025, when Ben desecrated Jacob in a horrific 17-3-2 butchering. With Jacob still nursing wounded arms like Tarik Skubal and Jesús Luzardo, Ben smells decay. One cold week from Jacob's bats, and the sixth seed will happily hammer the nails into his coffin.\n\n**No. 4 Brian vs. No. 5 Heidi**\n\nHeidi enters the postseason on life support. She has lost three consecutive matchups, capped by a Week 23 funeral where she posted a league-worst 63 grade and left all four transactions rotting on the vine. Awaiting her is Brian, who holds a 5-4-2 lifetime edge over her and took an 11-9-2 decision just two weeks ago. But the true trauma lies deeper in the crypt. Back in Week 10 of the 2023 campaign, Brian handed Heidi the ugliest beatdown of her managerial life, an unforgiving 16-4-2 mutilation. Heidi continues to nurse an infirmary lineup that carried Ceddanne Rafaela on the injured list all week. Brian may have stumbled against Austin, but Gavin Williams just struck out 24 men across two starts. If Heidi sleepwalks through the waiver wire again, Brian will bury her six feet deep before Sunday arrives.","bit":{"type":"matchups","name":"Upset Alert","data":[{"a":"Heidi","b":"Brian","kind":"CHAMPIONSHIP BRACKET","form_a":"L 7-13, L 4-17, L 9-11","form_b":"L 6-14, W 13-6, W 11-9","grade_a":101,"grade_b":104,"n":11,"rec":"Heidi 4, Brian 5, 2 tied","worst":"Brian won 16-4-2 (2023 week 10)","last":"Brian won 11-9-2 (2026 week 21)"},{"a":"Jacob","b":"Ben","kind":"CHAMPIONSHIP BRACKET","form_a":"W 11-9, W 12-7, L 9-10","form_b":"W 13-7, W 10-9, W 10-9","grade_a":108,"grade_b":98,"n":12,"rec":"Jacob 4, Ben 8","worst":"Ben won 17-3-2 (2025 week 12)","last":"Ben won 10-9-3 (2026 week 21)"}],"text":"Upset Alert: No. 6 Ben over No. 3 Jacob. Ben carries the momentum of three straight victories into the parlor, and Jacob has never truly cleansed the stench of that 17-3-2 slaughter from 2025."}},{"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","desk":"The Insider","color":"#6FB38A","headline":"INSIDE THE SMOKE-FILLED ROOMS: PLAYOFF TICKETS PUNCHED AND COMMISSIONER CRUSHED","dek":"Confidential sources spill all on Benny's catastrophic finale collapse, Andrew's record sprint, and the wild moves that shook up the bracket.","body":"Whispers through the back channels say the commissioner's suite is under complete lockdown. Just seven days after losing his grip on first place, Benny suffered a catastrophic 17-3-2 beatdown at the hands of Barak. Front office sources could barely believe their eyes as Rafael Devers, still technically listed on the injured list, torched baseballs for Barak to the tune of 6 home runs, 13 RBI, and a staggering 1.448 OPS.\n\nHow bad was the panic on Benny's bench? One league insider confirmed Benny left 3 unused waiver adds on the table, watched Gage Jump post a 9.72 ERA across two losses, and literally lost holds by a single hair (1 to 0) with a hold sitting untouched on his own bench. Doug Mercer called the upset last week, and sources say Barak's dugout was laughing all the way to the 135-grade romp.\n\nMeanwhile, in the celebratory champagne room, Andrew popped corks after a 15-6-1 rout of Gabe to officially lock up the regular season crown. Andrew's squad turned the basepaths into a personal playground, swiping 16 stolen bases. That ranks as the third-best single week in league history, according to high-ranking record keepers. Jacob deGrom set the tone with 13.0 pristine innings, fanning 15 while yielding a minuscule 0.46 WHIP.\n\nOver on the playoff bubble, the tension was thick enough to cut with a rusty cleat. Word out of Todd's camp is that the heartbreak is real. Todd posted a sparkling 123 grade behind Bryan Woo's magnificent 14.0 innings of 0.64 ERA ball, only to drop a painful 11-9-2 heartbreaker to Jacob. Todd lost stolen bases by one, holds by one, and quality starts 8 to 7, all while Matt Olson limped through a 1-for-24 freeze and 4 waiver adds went completely unused. That opened the door for Ben, who rode Ronald Acuña Jr. (3 homers, 9 RBI) to a 13-7-2 win over a sliding Heidi, clinching the sixth and final postseason berth on a three-matchup heater.\n\nLastly, while Austin snapped his skid with a 14-6-2 beating of Brian, scouts in the tunnel could only shake their heads at Brian's ice-cold stars: José Ramírez went 3-for-23 and Gunnar Henderson managed just 4-for-25. October is here, and the informants are ready.","bit":{"type":"rumor","name":"Trade Rumor of the Week","data":{"a":"Benny","b":"Brian","a_needs":"A","b_needs":"HR","a_gives":"Fernando Tatis Jr.","b_gives":"Gunnar Henderson","why":"Benny ranks 11% in A over the last four weeks while Brian sits at 88%; Brian is at 18% in HR where Benny is at 74% (share of all-play category wins)."},"text":"TRADE RUMOR OF THE WEEK: The deadline slammed shut on August 6, but winter whispers are already buzzing through league back rooms. Front office personnel note that Benny desperately needs assists, ranking at a dismal 11% all-play mark over the last month, while Brian ranks near the bottom at 18% in home runs. Sources suggest an offseason blockbuster could take shape around Fernando Tatis Jr. heading to Brian in exchange for Gunnar Henderson. Keep your ears to the ground."}}],"roster":["rosie","norm","seymour","doug","sauce"]},{"id":"2026-24","season":2026,"week":24,"date":"2026-09-14","phase":"playoffs","articles":[{"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","desk":"The Bright Side","color":"#E8B04B","headline":"Every Cloud Has A Golden Lining In The Postseason Sunshine!","dek":"Tough playoff battles build unstoppable champions for tomorrow!","body":"Welcome to playoff baseball, my wonderful friends! The postseason energy in The Frank Cup is simply electrifying, and every single pitch is packed with pure heart! While the scoreboard can be a strict teacher, remember that a stumble is just the first step in a brand new dance. Growth happens in the toughest moments, and there was so much glorious fight on display this week!\n\nTake our wonderful Heidi, for instance! Sure, Brian took the matchup 15-4-3 to advance, but you have to look deeper at the incredible foundation Heidi built on the mound! Her pitching staff earned a marvelous 85 grade, led by Noah Cameron tossing 12 sparkling innings of 0.75 ERA ball with two quality starts, and Ian Sam punching out 10 batters in six brilliant innings. Heidi locked down Errors, Saves, Quality Starts, and edged out Strikeout-to-Walk ratio at 2.81 to 2.76! Plus, Stolen Bases were decided by a single swipe (6 to 7) and Losses came down to a razor-thin 8 to 7 margin. That is championship caliber resilience, and Heidi has so much to hold her head high about!\n\nOver in the other quarterfinal, Ben showed the true heart of a competitor against Jacob! The final tally of 16-3-3 does not tell the real story of how fiercely Deputy Dangle battled. Ben captured Batting Average, Innings Pitched, and Strikeouts, powered by Randy Arozarena putting on an absolute clinic with three home runs and nine RBI! Out of the bullpen, Adrian Morejon was utterly flawless across four scoreless innings, picking up a save and three holds. Look at the margins, folks! Ben missed Triples by one (0 to 1), Stolen Bases by one (2 to 3), Pitching Wins by one (4 to 5), Holds by one (3 to 4), Quality Starts by one (4 to 5), and Losses by one (5 to 4). That is six categories decided by the thinnest sliver of luck! You cannot buy that kind of character development!\n\nMeanwhile, Brian and Jacob earned their tickets to the semifinals with dazzling displays of determination! Brian got an out-of-this-world week from Jackson Merrill, who batted .480 with three homers and 12 RBI, alongside Brice Turang batting .526! Jacob posted a magnificent 133 team grade, proving his lineup is clicking on all cylinders even after letting 25 strikeouts rest peacefully on the bench. \n\nWith Andrew and Benny waiting rested on their byes, the final four is set to be a pure joy! Keep smiling, keep believing, and remember that the best is always yet to come!","bit":{"type":"trivia","name":"Rosie's Trivia","data":{"question":"Which manager has beaten Gabe the most times in the regular season?","last_q":"Which manager has beaten Austin the most times in the regular season?","last_a":"Benny, 9 times."},"text":"Last week's trivia answer: It was Benny, who has defeated Austin 9 times in regular season play! Now for this week's brain-teaser: Which manager has beaten Gabe the most times in the regular season? Check back next week for the sunny answer!"}},{"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","desk":"The Numbers Desk","color":"#7FA8D6","headline":"The High Cost of Leaving Margins on the Table","dek":"While Brian and Jacob cruised into the semifinals, Heidi and Ben proved that leaving four adds on the wire is an excellent way to audit your own exit.","body":"Welcome to the postseason, where the sample sizes shrink to seven days and the margin for error approaches absolute zero. In the quarterfinals, we observed a masterclass in self-sabotage. If you ever want to explain the concept of opportunity cost to a child, simply show them the rosters of Heidi and Ben this week. Both managers were eliminated while leaving all four of their weekly acquisitions completely untouched. Let us consult the ledger.\n\nHeidi fell 15-4-3 to Brian, a result that looks like a rout but was actually governed by micro-margins. Heidi lost stolen bases by a single bag (7 to 6) and losses by one (8 to 7). Meanwhile, she kept Shohei Ohtani on her active roster while he was on the injured list for three days, leaving two empty hitter slot-days. The math does not care about your sentimentality; it cares about volume. To lose category margins this thin while refusing to utilize a single waiver add is not just bad luck; it is mathematically offensive. Brian, posting a magnificent 138 performance grade, did not need the help, but he certainly accepted the donation.\n\nThen we have Ben, whose 16-3-3 defeat at the hands of Jacob was a historical comedy of marginal errors. Doug Mercer predicted a Ben upset in last week's column, referencing Ben's 2025 dominance. Instead, Ben turned in a miserable 67-grade performance. Yet, the absurd truth is that Ben was agonizingly close. He lost triples 1 to 0, stolen bases 3 to 2, wins 5 to 4, losses 5 to 4, holds 4 to 3, and quality starts 5 to 4. That is six distinct categories decided by a single unit. With four adds left on the table, Ben essentially chose to observe his own elimination from the gallery.\n\nJacob, to his credit, advanced to the semifinals with a 133 grade, though not without his own computational errors. Jacob benched a spectacular 12-strikeout, nine-inning shutout from Jesús Luzardo. That single blunder cost Jacob the strikeout category, which he lost by 14 while leaving 25 strikeouts on his bench. Fortunately for him, Ben's roster was too inert to punish the mistake.\n\nThe bracket now shrinks, the variance narrows, and the remaining four managers must face the reality of the distribution. Those who do not manage their margins will find themselves writing columns about those who do.","bit":{"type":"odds","name":"The Odds Board","data":{"odds":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":56.9,"title":24.6},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":74.2,"title":45.7},"Jacob":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":43.1,"title":19.5},"Brian":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":25.8,"title":10.2}},"prev":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":59.5,"title":26.2},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":76.5,"title":50.5},"Jacob":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":30.3,"title":12.7},"Brian":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":17.2,"title":5.8},"Heidi":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":11.2,"title":3.5},"Ben":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":5.2,"title":1.3},"Austin":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Todd":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Barak":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0},"Gabe":{"po":0.0,"bye":0.0,"final":0.0,"title":0.0}},"phase":"playoffs"},"text":"The Odds Board reacts sharply to the quarterfinal results. Jacob is our biggest winner, climbing from a 12.7% title probability to 19.5% after dismantling Ben, while Brian nearly doubles his share to 10.2%. The most surprising figure remains Andrew's flat 100.0% bye probability turning into a modest 45.7% title projection. Despite dominating the regular season, the ruthless nature of bracket play means even the top seed has a 54.3% chance of going home empty-handed."}},{"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","desk":"The Roast Pit","color":"#D95B43","headline":"Postseason Punting of the Highest Order","dek":"Heidi and Ben leave their playoff rosters on autopilot, while Jacob survives his own bench blunders.","body":"Welcome to the playoffs, where the pressure separates the true contenders from the absolute clowns. Last week, I marked Heidi as an absolute fraud entering the postseason. I would like to formally apologize. She is not just a fraud; she is a masterclass in self-sabotage.\n\nHeidi bowed out of the championship bracket with a pathetic 15-4-3 loss to Brian. The tragedy is how easily it could have been avoided. Heidi managed to lose stolen bases seven to six and losses eight to seven, all while leaving four weekly roster adds completely unused on the table. To make things even sweeter, she started Shohei Ohtani's bat, Chase Burns, and Christian Scott while they were sitting on the injured list, plus she left two hitter slot-days completely empty. If you are trying to lose on purpose, you usually have to work a lot harder than that.\n\nBut do not worry, Heidi, you have company in the dumpster. Ben also made a swift exit from the postseason, getting dismantled 16-3-3 by Jacob, completely destroying Doug Mercer's genius prediction of a Ben upset. Ben also kept his hands in his pockets, leaving all four weekly adds on the table. He proceeded to lose triples one to zero, stolen bases three to two, wins five to four, losses four to five, holds four to three, and quality starts five to four. A single waiver-wire stream could have flipped any of those categories. Instead, Ben chose to go down with his inactive ship.\n\nJacob technically won, but his managerial decisions were equally brain-dead. He actively started four injured players, including Tarik Skubal, who was out for seven days. He also left a massive 25 strikeouts on his bench, highlighted by a gorgeous, complete-game gem from Jesús Luzardo (9.0 IP, 12 Ks, 0.00 ERA). Because of that benching, Jacob lost the strikeout category by 14. You won the matchup, Jacob, but your bench looks like a graveyard of missed opportunities.\n\nThe quarterfinals proved that while making the playoffs is a neat achievement, actually checking your lineup once you get there is apparently a monumental task.","bit":{"type":"dud","name":"Dud of the Week","data":{"name":"Anthony Molina","mlb":"SF","owner":"Heidi","line":"3.0 IP, 1 L, 4 K, 21.00 ERA, 3.67 WHIP"},"text":"It is time to present the crown of shame! The Dud of the Week goes to San Francisco pitcher Anthony Molina, proudly started by Heidi. Molina delivered a spectacular 21.00 ERA and a 3.67 WHIP over just 3.0 innings of work, earning a loss for his efforts. Congratulations, Heidi, your pitching staff truly caught fire, just not the good kind."}},{"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","desk":"Matchups to Watch","color":"#9A8FB8","headline":"The Grave Digs Deeper: Two Slain as the Semifinals Loom","dek":"Ben and Heidi leave their waiver adds in the casket, clearing the path for a brutal final four.","body":"The cold wind of the postseason has blown through our league's graveyard, leaving two more managers to rot in the consolation bracket. The soil is fresh, and the weeping has begun.\n\nFirst, I must confess my own blindness. Last week, I predicted that Ben would rise from the sixth seed to slay Jacob. Instead, Jacob delivered a merciless 16-3-3 thrashing. Ben went to his grave with four unused waiver acquisitions still clutched in his cold hands. He suffered the excruciating agony of losing six separate categories by a single hair: triples, stolen bases, wins, losses, holds, and quality starts. He starved with the keys to the pantry in his pocket. Jacob survived this round despite his own colossal blunder, leaving 25 strikeouts on his bench, including a complete-game masterpiece from Jesús Luzardo, which cost him the strikeout category by 14.\n\nMeanwhile, Heidi's postseason run ended in predictable horror, falling 15-4-3 to Brian. My colleague Sam Kessler rightly labeled her a fraud last week, and she proved it by leaving four waiver adds on the table and letting empty roster spots bleed her dry. She lost stolen bases 6 to 7 and losses 8 to 7 while starting three players on the injured list. Brian walked through her graveyard unscathed, propelled by a scorching Jackson Merrill, who went 12-for-25 with three home runs.\n\nNow, only four active managers remain to contest the crown. The semifinals will be a bloodbath.\n\nIn the first matchup, Benny faces Jacob. Benny holds a 6-3 all-time lead in this series, and Jacob surely still has nightmares about the terrifying 19-1-2 massacre Benny inflicted on him back in Week 4 of this very season. But Benny has been vulnerable lately, limping into his bye, while Jacob is riding a three-matchup winning streak.\n\nOn the other side, the top-seeded Andrew meets Brian. Their historic matchup is perfectly balanced at 5-5-1. Andrew surely remembers the glory of his 17-3-2 beatdown of Brian in Week 15 of 2025. But Brian's pitching has risen from the dead, and Andrew's Kyle Bradish just landed back on the injured list.\n\nOne week remains before the final clash. Two of these men will find their championship dreams buried in the frozen dirt by next Sunday. Prepare your eulogies.","bit":{"type":"matchups","name":"Upset Alert","data":[{"a":"Benny","b":"Jacob","kind":"CHAMPIONSHIP BRACKET","form_a":"L 3-17, L 7-12, W 10-8","form_b":"W 16-3, W 11-9, W 12-7","grade_a":110,"grade_b":109,"n":9,"rec":"Benny 6, Jacob 3","worst":"Benny won 19-1-2 (2026 week 4)","last":"Jacob won 12-7-3 (2026 week 22)"},{"a":"Austin","b":"Barak","kind":"consolation","form_a":"W 14-6, L 9-13, L 8-10","form_b":"W 17-3, W 13-9, W 12-9","grade_a":90,"grade_b":100,"n":10,"rec":"Austin 6, Barak 3, 1 tied","worst":"Austin won 15-5-2 (2024 week 14)","last":"Barak won 13-9-0 (2026 week 22)"},{"a":"Andrew","b":"Brian","kind":"CHAMPIONSHIP BRACKET","form_a":"W 15-6, W 17-4, L 9-11","form_b":"W 15-4, L 6-14, W 13-6","grade_a":113,"grade_b":105,"n":11,"rec":"Andrew 5, Brian 5, 1 tied","worst":"Andrew won 17-3-2 (2025 week 15)","last":"Brian won 13-6-3 (2026 week 17)"},{"a":"Todd","b":"Gabe","kind":"consolation","form_a":"L 9-11, L 9-10, W 11-9","form_b":"L 6-15, L 6-13, L 9-12","grade_a":97,"grade_b":81,"n":9,"rec":"Todd 5, Gabe 4","worst":"Gabe won 17-2-3 (2024 week 4)","last":"Todd won 16-4-2 (2026 week 17)"},{"a":"Heidi","b":"Ben","kind":"consolation","form_a":"L 4-15, L 7-13, L 4-17","form_b":"L 3-16, W 13-7, W 10-9","grade_a":99,"grade_b":97,"n":12,"rec":"Heidi 4, Ben 6, 2 tied","worst":"Ben won 17-2-3 (2025 week 4)","last":"Ben won 13-7-2 (2026 week 23)"}],"text":"Upset Alert: No. 3 Jacob over No. 2 Benny. Benny has spent weeks rotting since his late-season slide, and Jacob's offense is hungry enough to claw through the casket lid."}},{"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","desk":"The Insider","color":"#6FB38A","headline":"WAIVER WIRE WALKING DEAD: HEIDI AND BEN SINK IN PLAYOFF SHOCKER","dek":"A pair of four-add disasters doom the postseason dreams of two contenders as Jacob and Brian march on.","body":"The walls are closing in on the defeated, and the recriminations have already begun. Sources inside the front offices of both Heidi and Ben report total devastation after both managers suffered brutal quarterfinal exits while leaving a combined eight waiver additions completely unused on the wire. In the high-stakes environment of the Frank Cup playoffs, leaving weapons on the table is nothing short of fantasy malpractice.\n\nFor Heidi, the 4-15-3 defeat at the hands of Brian will haunt her front office all winter. A source close to the team whispered that the clubhouse is in complete disbelief after losing stolen bases by a single bag, 6 to 7, and losses by a microscopic margin of 8 to 7. With four unused waiver adds left to rot, Heidi essentially threw away the keys to the semifinals. Brian did not need the help, but he gladly took it, riding a wave of absolute brilliance from Jackson Merrill, who torched opposing pitching for a 12-for-25 week with three home runs and 12 RBIs.\n\nMeanwhile, the scene in Ben's war room was even more tragic. Despite Doug Mercer confidently predicting that Ben would pull off a monumental upset over Jacob, the defending champion was absolutely dismantled 16-3-3. Highly placed sources confirm that Ben's management simply froze under the pressure. He lost triples by one, stolen bases by one, wins by one, losses by one, holds by one, and quality starts by one. Six separate categories were decided by the absolute slimmest of margins, all while Ben sat on four unused adds that could have easily tipped the balance of power.\n\nJacob will march on to the semifinals, but league insiders are quietly snickering at his preparation. Jacob somehow managed to lose the strikeout category by 14 despite leaving a mind-boggling 25 strikeouts on his bench. That included a brilliant Monday performance by Jesús Luzardo, who tossed nine scoreless innings with 12 strikeouts while locked in an inactive slot. \n\nWith Benny resting on his bye week, watching his Week 23 acquisitions Carson Benge and Coby Mayo combine for five home runs, the remaining contenders are on high alert. The regular season champion, Andrew, is waiting in the wings, and the margin for error has officially shrunk to zero.","bit":{"type":"rumor","name":"Trade Rumor of the Week","data":{"a":"Brian","b":"Gabe","a_needs":"AVG","b_needs":"SV","a_gives":"Aroldis Chapman","b_gives":"Alex Bregman","why":"Brian ranks 25% in AVG over the last four weeks while Gabe sits at 85%; Gabe is at 7% in SV where Brian is at 78% (share of all-play category wins)."},"text":"Whispers are already circulating about a potential blockbuster offseason swap between Brian and Gabe. League sources indicate Brian is desperate for batting average while Gabe is starved for saves. The rumored framework has Brian sending Aroldis Chapman to Gabe in exchange for Alex Bregman, a classic win-win for both franchises to chew on before the keeper deadline."}}],"roster":["rosie","norm","seymour","doug","sauce"]},{"id":"2026-25","season":2026,"week":25,"date":"2026-09-21","phase":"playoffs","articles":[{"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","desk":"The Bright Side","color":"#E8B04B","headline":"Triples, Triumphs, and the Beautiful Radiance of Postseason Grit!","dek":"Though only two managers advance to the grand finale, every single matchup this week was a gorgeous testament to the power of friendship and fantasy baseball!","body":"Oh, what a breathtaking week of postseason magic in The Frank Cup! The sun is shining, the autumn breeze is crisp, and our hearts are absolutely bursting with pride for all ten of our incredible managers. We have officially crowned our two finalists, but let us be absolutely clear: every single manager who competed this week is a champion in spirit!\n\nFirst, let us extend a massive, joyful congratulations to Andrew! Not only did he secure a ticket to the grand finale with a 12-9-1 victory over Brian, but he treated us to a historic display of pure hustle. Andrew's squad zipped around the bases for an all-time league record of 7 triples! Simply magnificent! But let us not overlook Brian's spectacular effort. Brian lost WHIP by a microscopic margin of 1.14 to 1.15. While some might focus on those two unused waiver additions, I see a manager who trusted his core and fell just a whisper short of glory. With Gavin Williams and Yoshinobu Yamamoto turning in beautiful, scoreless gems, Brian has laid a gorgeous foundation for a spectacular 2027!\n\nOver in the other semifinal, Benny proved why he is a two-time champion, securing a 16-4-2 win over Jacob. Our colleague Doug Mercer predicted a Jacob upset last week, and while the scoreboard looks wide, the actual matchup was a beautifully tense thriller! Jacob was just a single stolen base (5 to 6), one hold (0 to 1), and one quality start (5 to 6) away from turning this matchup completely on its head. Tarik Skubal's glorious 7-inning, 9-strikeout shutout on Monday was a work of art, and Trevor Rogers gave us a breathtaking 10-strikeout masterpiece on Saturday. Keep your chin high, Jacob! Your squad showed the heart of a lion.\n\nEven in our consolation bracket, the silver linings were absolutely dazzling! Yes, Ben fell to Heidi in a 10-9-3 heartbreaker despite actually having the higher weekly grade of 82 to 72. That is just baseball's way of wrapping us in a warm embrace and reminding us to appreciate the journey! Ben left an astounding 46 strikeouts and two wins on his bench, which only proves his roster is bursting at the seams with untapped excellence!\n\nAnd to Barak and Gabe, who both left a few spots empty or had superstars like Aaron Judge and Rafael Devers on the injured list, remember: resting your heroes is the ultimate act of leadership. The future is so incredibly bright, my friends. Let us cheer extra loud as Andrew and Benny face off for the crown!","bit":{"type":"trivia","name":"Rosie's Trivia","data":{"question":"Which manager holds the league record for most BB in a single week, and how many?","last_q":"Which manager has beaten Gabe the most times in the regular season?","last_a":"Benny, 10 times."},"text":"Rosie's Trivia: Last week, we asked which manager has beaten Gabe the most times in the regular season. The answer is Benny, with 10 victories! This week's question: Which manager holds the league record for most BB in a single week, and how many?"}},{"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","desk":"The Numbers Desk","color":"#7FA8D6","headline":"The Margin of Error Approaches Zero: Semifinal Math and Self-Sabotage","dek":"While Andrew sets historic records, Benny exposes the perils of faulty predictions and empty rosters.","body":"In the high-stakes environment of the fantasy semifinals, the law of large numbers always exacts its toll. We must begin by addressing the ongoing forecasting crisis at the Gazette. Last week, our colleague Doug Mercer boldly predicted that Jacob would upset Benny. The actual mathematical output? Benny dismantled Jacob by a score of 16-4-2.\n\nBenny put up a robust 129 grade and an 8-1-0 all-play record, while Jacob cratered to a grade of 69. Jacob, who was previously warned by Sam Kessler about starting injured players, once again left inactive assets in his lineup, starting Tarik Skubal and Troy Melton. Jacob also left one waiver add unused. Consequently, he lost stolen bases (6 to 5), holds (1 to 0), and quality starts (6 to 5) by the thinnest of margins. Regression is a harsh teacher, but negligence is even harsher.\n\nBenny's offense was carried by Fernando Tatis Jr., who blasted four home runs, offsetting a cold week from Luis Garcia Jr. (2-for-21) and a disastrous performance from Tanner Bibee, who posted an 11.57 ERA.\n\nOn the other side of the bracket, Andrew demonstrated why he finished the regular season in first place. He posted a spectacular 136 grade and a flawless 9-0-0 all-play record to defeat Brian 12-9-1. Andrew was propelled by hot hitting from Pete Crow-Armstrong, who went 10-for-29 with four home runs, and David Peterson, who provided 12.0 stellar innings with a 1.50 ERA. Andrew even established a new league record by hitting 7 triples in a single week, eclipsing Ben's 2023 mark of 6. Brian fought admirably with a 120 grade, but his 1.15 WHIP fell just short of Andrew's 1.14. Leaving two adds unused when a single streaming pitcher could have flipped WHIP is an inefficiency that will haunt Brian's offseason.\n\nMeanwhile, in the consolation bracket, the epidemic of bench blunders reached historic proportions. Ben, who fell to Heidi despite having a superior overall grade (82 to 72), left an astonishing 46 strikeouts on his bench. Barak managed an even more impressive mathematical tragedy, losing wins by one and strikeouts by 27 while leaving 4 wins and 30 strikeouts sitting on his bench.\n\nWe now head to a highly logical championship matchup between the top two seeds. Andrew holds the statistical edge, but as we have seen, empty roster slots and unused transactions can easily defy the projection models.","bit":{"type":"odds","name":"The Odds Board","data":{"odds":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":100.0,"title":37.9},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":100.0,"title":62.1}},"prev":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":56.9,"title":24.6},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":74.2,"title":45.7},"Jacob":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":43.1,"title":19.5},"Brian":{"po":100.0,"bye":0.0,"final":25.8,"title":10.2}},"phase":"playoffs"},"text":"With the bracket officially narrowed to two, our Monte Carlo simulator has crowned Andrew as the heavy favorite, with his title odds surging from 45.7% to 62.1%. Benny also enjoyed a significant upward adjustment, moving from a 24.6% chance to 37.9%. The most surprising metric remains Benny's lower title probability despite his historical dominance as a two-time champion, proving that the model respects Andrew's superior regular season sample size."}},{"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","desk":"The Roast Pit","color":"#D95B43","headline":"Semifinal Slaughter: Andrew Rolls, Benny Coasting, and Waiver Wire Ghosts","dek":"Brian and Jacob hand over their championship tickets while Gabe sets new standards for bench-warming incompetence.","body":"I love being right, but watching Doug Mercer be catastrophically wrong is an even sweeter delicacy. Doug predicted Jacob would upset Benny in the semifinals. Instead, Benny handed Jacob a 16-4-2 shellacking that belonged in an emergency room. Jacob decided the best strategy for a semifinal matchup was leaving an unused add in his pocket while losing stolen bases 6-5, holds 1-0, and quality starts 6-5. Even better, Jacob spent the week starting Tarik Skubal and Troy Melton while both were on the injured list. You cannot make this level of managerial negligence up, folks.\n\nOver in the other semifinal, top-seeded Andrew secured his spot in the championship with a 12-9-1 victory over Brian. Andrew did not just win; he hit a league-record seven triples in a single week. Seven! Meanwhile, Brian gave a masterclass in giving up. He left two waiver adds unused while watching Andrew steal the WHIP category by a razor-thin margin of 1.14 to 1.15. One single relief streamer could have flipped that category and sent Brian to the finals, but setting a lineup apparently takes too many clicks.\n\nIf you thought the championship bracket had exclusive rights to malpractice, the consolation ladder held its own festival of failure. Ben managed to lose 10-9-3 to Heidi despite posting an 82 grade to her miserable 72. How do you lose to a team that scored a 72? Easy: leave four waiver adds on the shelf, lose stolen bases 3-2, and leave forty-six strikeouts rotting on your bench.\n\nNot to be outdone, Gabe lost 16-5-1 to Todd by leaving four adds unused while dropping home runs by one, wins by one, holds by one, quality starts by one, and ERA 4.20 to 4.32. Gabe also parked twenty-three strikeouts, two wins, and a quality start on his bench. That is not just losing; that is an art form of active surrender.\n\nSo here we are. Andrew versus Benny for the title. Two regular season powerhouses who actually look at their screens before locking lineups. Let us see if Benny can refrain from starting injured players long enough to prevent Andrew from walking away with the cup.","bit":{"type":"dud","name":"Dud of the Week","data":{"name":"Kyle Leahy","mlb":"STL","owner":"Gabe","line":"3.0 IP, 1 L, 1 K, 18.00 ERA, 2.67 WHIP"},"text":"The Dud of the Week award goes to Kyle Leahy of the St. Louis Cardinals, proudly deployed in Gabe's active lineup. Leahy coughed up an 18.00 ERA and a 2.67 WHIP over three horrific innings, tallying one loss and just a single strikeout. Congratulations on single-handedly incinerating Gabe's pitching categories."}},{"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","desk":"Matchups to Watch","color":"#9A8FB8","headline":"Two Open Graves: Benny and Andrew Face the Final Descent","dek":"As the championship matchup is set, the fantasy baseball underworld prepares for its final, cold embrace.","body":"Once more, my dark vision was clouded. Last week, I foretold that Jacob would rise to strike down Benny in the semifinals. Instead, Benny constructed a massive 16-4-2 tomb for Jacob, leaving him to rot in the cold earth. Jacob sealed his own fate, leaving one crucial waiver addition unused while losing stolen bases, holds, and quality starts by the thinnest of margins. He even rolled out Tarik Skubal and Troy Melton while both lay on the injured list. It was a slow, self-inflicted execution.\n\nMeanwhile, Andrew dismantled Brian 12-9-1 to claim his place in the final resting place. Andrew rode a historic wave of fortune, setting an all-time league record with seven triples. Pete Crow-Armstrong paced the onslaught, tormenting Brian's pitchers with four home runs. Brian watched his season crumble by a whisper, losing WHIP 1.14 to 1.15, all while leaving two roster additions unused on the table. Neglect is a cold, indifferent killer in the postseason.\n\nIn the lower depths of the consolation bracket, Heidi somehow escaped with a 10-9-3 victory over Ben, despite posting a ghastly 72 grade to Ben's 82. The fraudulence I warned you about persists, sustained only by Ben's own refusal to utilize his four waiver adds. Elsewhere, Todd dismantled Gabe 16-5-1, as Gabe left four additions on the table and lost five different categories by a hair.\n\nAnd so, the final two stand upon the precipice of eternity. Benny and Andrew. Benny, the two-time champion from 2023 and 2024, seeks to reclaim his dark throne. Andrew, the regular-season ruler, wants his first crown. Their history is stained with ancient blood. While Andrew took their last meeting in Week 18, Benny holds the all-time edge 6 to 4.\n\nDo not forget the darkest chapter of their rivalry: Week 18 of 2024, when Benny dragged Andrew into the deep with an ungodly 14-4-4 massacre. Only one of these titans will survive the week. The other will be buried in a shallow, unmarked grave.\n\nIn the minor burial plots, Jacob and Brian meet to settle old scores. Jacob must face the phantom of Week 20, 2025, when Brian inflicted a horrifying 17-1-4 beatdown. Meanwhile, Barak and Gabe battle to see who crawls out of the cellar, dredging up memories of Gabe's 17-4-1 slaughter of Barak back in Week 10 of 2023. Prepare your souls. The end is nigh.","bit":{"type":"matchups","name":"Upset Alert","data":[{"a":"Benny","b":"Andrew","kind":"CHAMPIONSHIP BRACKET","form_a":"W 16-4, L 3-17, L 7-12","form_b":"W 12-9, W 15-6, W 17-4","grade_a":111,"grade_b":114,"n":10,"rec":"Benny 6, Andrew 4","worst":"Benny won 14-4-4 (2024 week 18)","last":"Andrew won 11-9-2 (2026 week 18)"},{"a":"Austin","b":"Todd","kind":"consolation","form_a":"W 10-9, W 14-6, L 9-13","form_b":"W 16-5, L 9-11, L 9-10","grade_a":91,"grade_b":98,"n":11,"rec":"Austin 5, Todd 6","worst":"Todd won 16-3-3 (2026 week 18)","last":"Todd won 16-3-3 (2026 week 18)"},{"a":"Barak","b":"Gabe","kind":"consolation","form_a":"L 9-10, W 17-3, W 13-9","form_b":"L 5-16, L 6-15, L 6-13","grade_a":100,"grade_b":80,"n":11,"rec":"Barak 5, Gabe 6","worst":"Gabe won 17-4-1 (2023 week 10)","last":"Barak won 12-9-1 (2026 week 21)"},{"a":"Jacob","b":"Brian","kind":"consolation","form_a":"L 4-16, W 16-3, W 11-9","form_b":"L 9-12, W 15-4, L 6-14","grade_a":107,"grade_b":106,"n":11,"rec":"Jacob 3, Brian 7, 1 tied","worst":"Brian won 17-1-4 (2025 week 20)","last":"Jacob won 11-8-3 (2026 week 19)"}],"text":"UPSET ALERT: Todd over Austin. Austin's phenomenal run will end in cold ashes as Todd's offense, fresh off scoring 16 categories against Gabe, drags him into the dirt."}},{"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","desk":"The Insider","color":"#6FB38A","headline":"SEMIFINAL SHOCKERS: ANDREW SETS ALL-TIME RECORD WHILE BENNY DESTROYS JACOB'S FINALS DREAMS","dek":"As the championship matchup is set, front office whispers reveal how roster neglect and historic triples paved the way to the Frank Cup final.","body":"League sources are buzzing. The championship matchup is set. It is Andrew versus Benny. The two regular-season heavyweights have advanced, but the path was paved with historic feats and absolute back-office malpractice.\n\nLet us start in Franzia's front office. Andrew did not just beat Brian 12-9-1; he did so with historic flair. People familiar with the roster confirm Andrew set an all-time league record with 7 triples this week, shattering Ben's 2023 mark of 6. Pete Crow-Armstrong (10-for-29, 4 HR) and Miguel Vargas (3 HR, 9 RBI) absolutely lit up the scoreboard. A front office official close to Brian was seen weeping after losing WHIP by a microscopic margin of 1.15 to 1.14. The kicker? Brian left two weekly additions completely unused on the waiver wire while losing that category by a hair.\n\nOver in Benny's camp, The Commissioner 2x absolutely dismantled Jacob 16-4-2, completely embarrassing my colleague Doug Mercer, who had foolishly predicted a Jacob upset. A source close to Jacob's dugout described the vibe as pure chaos, especially after Jacob left one crucial add on the table. That single unused move proved fatal, as Jacob lost stolen bases (6 to 5), holds (1 to 0), and quality starts (6 to 5) all by a hair. Jacob's grade of 69 was an absolute eyesore.\n\nMeanwhile, the consolation bracket was a masterclass in benching blunders. Ben left four adds unused and lost 10-9-3 to Heidi by a single stolen base, despite keeping an astonishing 46 strikeouts sitting on his bench. Sources also confirm that Barak lost wins by one with four wins sitting on his bench, while leaving four adds unused. Gabe's front office showed similar levels of neglect, leaving four adds on the table while losing home runs, wins, holds, ERA, and quality starts to Todd by the slimmest of margins, all while leaving two wins and a quality start on his bench.\n\nNow, the final stage is set. Benny is looking to reclaim his crown, while regular-season champion Andrew is riding a three-match win streak. The championship battle promises to be an absolute war.","bit":{"type":"rumor","name":"Trade Rumor of the Week","data":{"a":"Benny","b":"Gabe","a_needs":"A","b_needs":"SV","a_gives":"Riley O'Brien","b_gives":"Ozzie Albies","why":"Benny ranks 11% in A over the last four weeks while Gabe sits at 74%; Gabe is at 9% in SV where Benny is at 67% (share of all-play category wins)."},"text":"TRADE RUMOR OF THE WEEK: Front office spies whisper that Benny and Gabe are already laying the groundwork for an offseason blockbuster. Benny, who ranked in the 11th percentile in assists over the last month, is looking to acquire Ozzie Albies. In exchange, Gabe would receive Riley O'Brien to shore up a bullpen that ranked in the 9th percentile in saves. Though the August 6 trade deadline has long passed, sources say this keeper-minded swap is very much on the table for the winter."}}],"roster":["rosie","norm","seymour","doug","sauce"]}],"ledger":[{"season":2026,"week":22,"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","line":"Declared Heidi primed for a playoff run despite a 17-4-1 loss; predicted Austin's luck is due to flip after an unlucky 121-grade performance."},{"season":2026,"week":22,"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","line":"Recorded Austin's eight-matchup losing streak and his fatal benching of a pitching win against Barak. Noted Ben holding an 82.8% playoff probability over Todd (17.2%) entering the Week 23 regular season finale."},{"season":2026,"week":22,"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","line":"Mocked Benny for coughing up first place by starting five injured players against Jacob. Put Austin on blast for an eight-game losing streak sealed by leaving a win on his bench against Barak."},{"season":2026,"week":22,"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","line":"Predicted Barak will upset Benny in Week 23, denying Benny a chance to reclaim the regular season crown. Warned Todd and Heidi that their pitching collapses will cost them their seasons."},{"season":2026,"week":22,"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","line":"Andrew took the top seed from Benny after thirteen weeks of dominance; predicted Austin's eight-week losing streak will drag him entirely out of contention due to inexcusable bench errors."},{"season":2026,"week":23,"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","line":"Celebrated Benny securing a first-round bye despite a Week 23 loss to Barak, highlighted Austin's streak-busting win as proof of a bright future, and congratulated Ben and Heidi on reaching the postseason."},{"season":2026,"week":23,"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","line":"Recorded Andrew clinching the regular season crown with the #3 all-time stolen base week (16 SB). Confirmed Ben clinching the final playoff spot over Todd via model projection."},{"season":2026,"week":23,"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","line":"Roasted Benny for blowing the regular season title with inactive IL players and roasted Todd for missing the playoffs with four unused adds. Marked Heidi as an absolute fraud entering the postseason after an 0-9-0 all-play week."},{"season":2026,"week":23,"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","line":"Predicted No. 6 Ben to upset No. 3 Jacob in the opening round, reminding everyone of Ben's 17-3-2 beatdown in 2025. Noted Benny limping into his bye after getting crushed 17-3-2 by Barak as predicted."},{"season":2026,"week":23,"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","line":"Reported on Benny's total collapse against Barak and Andrew's historic 16-steal week to secure the top seed. Floated an offseason blockbuster rumor linking Benny's Fernando Tatis Jr. to Brian for Gunnar Henderson."},{"season":2026,"week":24,"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","line":"Rosie praised Heidi and Ben for narrow category losses in their quarterfinal exits, highlighting Randy Arozarena's hot bat and Noah Cameron's pitching as foundational building blocks for next season."},{"season":2026,"week":24,"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","line":"Recorded Heidi and Ben getting eliminated in the quarterfinals due to catastrophic waiver-wire neglect. Noted Doug Mercer' failed prediction of a Ben upset over Jacob."},{"season":2026,"week":24,"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","line":"Mocked Heidi and Ben for wasting all four weekly additions while losing tight playoff categories. Noted Jacob's massive benching of Jesús Luzardo's shutout and warned him about continuing to start injured players."},{"season":2026,"week":24,"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","line":"Admitted I was wrong about Ben upsetting Jacob. Maintained that Heidi was a fraud as Sam Kessler predicted. Predicted Jacob to upset Benny in the semifinals."},{"season":2026,"week":24,"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","line":"Called out Heidi and Ben for historic playoff collapses fueled by leaving four waiver adds unused. Mocked Doug Mercer for his failed prediction of a Ben upset over Jacob."},{"season":2026,"week":25,"writer":"rosie","name":"Rosie Callahan","line":"Rosie highlighted Benny and Andrew advancing to the championship, comforted Jacob after a narrow semifinal loss, and celebrated Ben's bench depth despite a luck-alert loss to Heidi."},{"season":2026,"week":25,"writer":"norm","name":"Norm Becker","line":"Recorded Doug Mercer' second consecutive failed playoff prediction, as Benny easily defeated Jacob. Noted Andrew's record-setting 7 triples and established Andrew as the statistical favorite for the championship over Benny."},{"season":2026,"week":25,"writer":"seymour","name":"Sam Kessler","line":"Sam Kessler: Called out Doug Mercer for his failed Jacob-over-Benny prediction after Benny smashed Jacob 16-4-2. Roasted Brian, Jacob, Ben, and Gabe for leaving vital adds unused in narrow category losses."},{"season":2026,"week":25,"writer":"doug","name":"Doug Mercer","line":"Admitted I was wrong about Jacob upsetting Benny in the semifinals. Maintained Heidi's fraud status despite her advancing. Highlighted the historical 2024 beatdown between Benny and Andrew ahead of their championship clash."},{"season":2026,"week":25,"writer":"sauce","name":"Tony Russo","line":"Called out Jacob, Brian, Ben, and Gabe for leaving crucial waiver additions unused during tight playoff matchups. Mocked Doug Mercer for his failed prediction of a Jacob upset over Benny."}],"trivia_used":["pick1-2024","h2h-Austin","h2h-Gabe","rec-BB"],"trivia_log":{"2026-22":{"id":"pick1-2024","q":"Who went first overall in the 2024 draft, and which manager took him?","a":"Ronald Acuña Jr., taken by Jacob."},"2026-23":{"id":"h2h-Austin","q":"Which manager has beaten Austin the most times in the regular season?","a":"Benny, 9 times."},"2026-24":{"id":"h2h-Gabe","q":"Which manager has beaten Gabe the most times in the regular season?","a":"Benny, 10 times."},"2026-25":{"id":"rec-BB","q":"Which manager holds the league record for most BB in a single week, and how many?","a":"Barak, with 58 BB in 2023 week 1."}},"trivia_open":{"id":"rec-BB","q":"Which manager holds the league record for most BB in a single week, and how many?","a":"Barak, with 58 BB in 2023 week 1."},"updated":"2026-09-26T14:09:05+00:00","odds_prev":{"Benny":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":100.0,"title":37.9},"Andrew":{"po":100.0,"bye":100.0,"final":100.0,"title":62.1}},"rumor_prev":["Benny","Gabe"],"migrations":["names2"],"daily":[{"date":"2026-09-22","season":2026,"week":26,"writer":"buck","name":"Buck Harrington","desk":"The Morning Wire","color":"#E07A3F","headline":"Morning Wire: Championship Chaos Erupts in Week 26","body":"Good morning sports fans! This is Buck Harrington waking up The Frank Cup Gazette with the pulse-pounding truth of Week 26! \n\n**Big Bats** CJ Abrams came to play for Benny on September 22nd, going two for four with two monstrous home runs, three RBIs, and two runs scored! \n\n**Mound Masterpieces** Andrew got a huge start from Zack Wheeler, who punched out eleven batters across five and two-thirds innings! \n\n**League Moves** Benny made a bullpen splash, adding relief pitcher Jeff Hoffman while cutting Landen Roupp loose! \n\n**The MLB Wire** Blade Tidwell hits the fifteen day injured list for Andrew, while Benny holds Connor Prielipp and Cristian Javier, the latter battling through Houston injuries! Andrew counts on Chris Bassitt as Max Scherzer hits start number five hundred, plus Parker Messick keeps turning heads for Andrew! \n\n**The Scoreboard** In the championship bracket with five days left, Benny and Andrew are locked in a blistering seven to eight to seven tie! Tight categories show wins at zero to one and losses at two to one! \n\nThat is all for today! This is Buck Harrington signing off for the morning wire!","ledger":"Benny and Andrew are tied 7-8-7 in a fierce championship battle while Zack Wheeler punches out 11."},{"date":"2026-09-23","season":2026,"week":26,"writer":"buck","name":"Buck Harrington","desk":"The Morning Wire","color":"#E07A3F","headline":"Morning Wire: Championship Battles Roar in Week 26","body":"This is Buck Harrington and you are dialed in to The Morning Wire for The Frank Cup Gazette! We are hurtling toward the finish line of Week 26 and the championship bracket is an absolute thriller! \n\n**Big Bats** \nBen Rice! Stop the presses! The Yankee slugger detonated for three hits, two titanic homers, and two RBIs for Benny! And how about Henry Bolte chipping in four hits and crossing the dish twice! Absolute fireworks in the Bronx and beyond!\n\n**The Wire** \n roster turbulence hits the wire! Benny loses right-hander Cristian Javier to the fifteen-day injured list. Over in Andrew's camp, the Nationals optioned lefty Jackson Kent to Rochester, while the Yankees might hold Cy Young frontrunner Cam Schlittler out of Game One in the wild-card series! Meanwhile, George Kirby logged negative win probability added in a tough loss to Seattle.\n\n**Scoreboard Watch** \nLet us check the championship clash with four days left to play! Benny holds a razor-thin eleven to eight edge over Andrew with three ties, led by a tight battle where Andrew leads in at-bats twelve to eleven and losses three to two! \n\nThat is all for today, sports fans. Keep your bats high and your dial locked right here on The Frank Cup Gazette!","ledger":"Benny holds an eleven to eight lead over Andrew in the championship bracket as Ben Rice crushes two home runs."},{"date":"2026-09-24","season":2026,"week":26,"writer":"buck","name":"Buck Harrington","desk":"The Morning Wire","color":"#E07A3F","headline":"Morning Wire: Crow-Armstrong Hits 40-40 as Championship Battle Tightens","body":"Rise and shine, baseball fans! Buck Harrington here, beaming this high-octane update straight to your speakers! We are coming down to the wire in the championship battle and the drama is at an absolute fever pitch!\n\n**Big Days** History has been made! Pete Crow-Armstrong of Andrew's squad has officially joined the elite 40-40 club, swiping his 40th bag in a season for the ages! Not to be outdone, Andrew's ace Cam Schlittler wrapped up a spectacular, Cy Young caliber regular season with a final start on Thursday night to push the Yankees to victory!\n\n**Moves** Benny is shuffling the deck chairs in the final stretch! Benny added hurlers Dean Kremer and Robert Gasser to the roster, while cutting ties with Connor Prielipp, Max Fried, and Cristian Javier! It is a pure arms race out there!\n\n**MLB Wire** Bad news on the health front for Andrew! Pitcher Kyle Bradish has been moved to the 60-day injured list with lower abdominal discomfort, and hurler Blade Tidwell joins him there with a right UCL sprain! Meanwhile, the award buzz is deafening as Pete Crow-Armstrong targets MVP honors and Sal Stewart makes his case for Rookie of the Year!\n\n**Matchups** It is coming down to the final three days in the championship matchup! Benny holds a razor-thin 9-8-5 lead over Andrew! We are looking at a dead heat in Quality Starts with Andrew clinging to a microscopic 1-0 advantage! Every single inning counts! This is Buck Harrington, signing off!","ledger":"Andrew's Pete Crow-Armstrong enters the historic 40-40 club while Benny makes heavy roster moves to maintain a narrow lead in the championship matchup."},{"date":"2026-09-25","season":2026,"week":26,"writer":"buck","name":"Buck Harrington","desk":"The Morning Wire","color":"#E07A3F","headline":"Morning Wire: Murakami Bombs and deGrom Disaster Shake Up Championship Fight","body":"Good morning, fantasy baseball faithful! Turn up the dial because the Frank Cup championship final is coming right down to the wire!\n\n**Big Bats** set the airwaves ablaze on Friday! Benny got a massive boost from Munetaka Murakami, who put on an absolute spectacle, going 3-for-4 with two colossal home runs, three RBI, and two runs scored. Andrew refused to back down, getting a monster performance from Roman Anthony, who went 4-for-6 with three runs scored and a pair of walks!\n\n**Mound Meltdowns** sent shockwaves through the title fight! Andrew took a heavy blow as Jacob deGrom got obliterated for an 18.00 ERA and a 2.75 WHIP over just four innings, taking the loss and torpedoing crucial pitching ratios!\n\n**The Wire** was active as the clock ticks down! Andrew reshuffled his pitching staff by adding Jonah Tong and cutting loose Jackson Kent. Out in MLB action, Benny cheered on Robert Gasser, who delivered six strong innings for Milwaukee, while Tanner Scott anchored the Dodgers bullpen. Meanwhile, Andrew watched David Peterson take a tough loss despite a quality effort, while Jake McCarthy kept pushing toward a rare 15-15-15-15 season in Colorado.\n\n**Scoreboard** alert as we head into the weekend! Benny holds an 11-8-3 lead over Andrew in the championship bracket with just two days left! Categories like runs, saves, holds, and ERA are hanging by a thread, so stay tuned, sports fans! Buck Harrington signing off!","ledger":"Munetaka Murakami's explosive two-homer night gives Benny breathing room as Jacob deGrom's meltdown opens up pitching categories for Andrew to reclaim."}]};
